    <div class="col-text">
      <div class="col-lede">She was modern. She was beautiful. She was <em>a problem.</em></div>
      <p class="col-body drop-cap">
        In interwar Vietnam, a new figure emerged across newspapers, advertisements, and satirical cartoons: the <em>Gái Mới</em> — the "new girl." She wore fitted dresses and bathing suits. She read French novels. She went to school. And she made everyone furious.
      </p>
      <p class="col-body">
        This thesis asks why the body of a young Vietnamese woman became the central arena for debates about modernity, nationalism, and resistance under French colonial rule — and what her image tells us about who got to define "progress" in 1930s Vietnam.
      </p>
      <div class="pull-quote">
        <p>"Why was she such a <em>hot topic</em> across every publication?"</p>
        <cite>— Julia Do, Harvard Hist. & Lit. Senior Spotlight, 2025</cite>
      </div>
      <p class="col-body">
        Working across women's periodicals, beauty ads, op-eds, and girls' school records, I trace how the <em>Gái Mới</em> was simultaneously mocked by traditionalists, celebrated by reformers, and weaponized by anti-colonial writers — all at once.
      </p>
    </div>
